diff --git a/docs/HLSD/INDX.md b/docs/HLSD/INDX.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..fc95069a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/HLSD/INDX.md
@@ -0,0 +1,84 @@
+# INDX personal filament library
+
+The INDX GUI button links an Orca printer with a configurable number of passive tools to the printer's Moonraker configuration directory. The tools share a Klipper feeder and heater; tool selection remains part of the printer's CHANGE_TOOL macro. The feature is gated by INDX_PRINT_START in the printer start script and 1-64 nozzle diameters (the Orca software bound). The G-code writer recognizes INDX_PRINT_START in the printer start script.
+
+Orca publishes the loaded standard and personal filament catalogue as compressed schema-2 JSON. A source is identified by its exact preset name, with a SHA-1 revision of its resolved configuration. The uploaded summary includes material settings needed by the touchscreen. Full configuration cloning stays inside Orca, preserving source-specific settings without sending the whole preset collection to the printer. A request token makes the touchscreen's refresh wait for a new publication rather than reuse stale data.
+
+The touchscreen owns a schema-3 personal library in indx-filament-library.json. Stable UUIDs identify physical filament profiles independently of editable names. Profiles hold source references, brand, material, colour, temperatures and default flow settings. Calibration records and manual tool overrides are indexed by tool number, with the nozzle diameter on each calibration. PA, flow ratio and maximum volumetric speed therefore remain distinct for different passive nozzles. Generic catalogue sources and personal calibrated profiles have separate touchscreen lists.
+
+Orca's background publisher also imports the personal library when the filament editor has no unsaved edits. It saves detached user presets through PresetCollection, using a stable INDX filament identity for each library UUID and tool. Generated presets are excluded from the source catalogue to prevent feedback loops. Explicit sync additionally applies the assigned profiles and solid colours to the open project and sets manual filament mapping for the configured tool count on the current plate. Idle background imports apply the assigned profiles and colors to their tool slots. Re-slicing is required to use changed material settings.
+
+INDX G-code sends PA to the shared Klipper extruder. An unenabled PA explicitly resets the shared feeder's previous value. Temperature commands address only the selected logical filament and omit a physical T parameter, while parked passive nozzles receive no preheat commands. Other printer configurations retain their existing behavior.
+
+Touchscreen test generation and all physical calibration remain guarded by the printer's commissioning macros. Flow and throughput tests are visually evaluated; the load cell is not used to infer their results. Software synchronization does not enable commissioning gates or flash firmware.
+
+The nozzle dialog saves an independent diameter and Standard/CHT choice for each tool. Calibration variants use a C-locale key containing tool number, diameter to three decimal places, and nozzle type. Legacy tool-only records remain eligible only when their original diameter and Standard nozzle match. A generic CHT estimate uses a configurable multiplier, initially 1.30; a measured maximum flow overrides it. Generated source references prevent repeated nozzle changes from multiplying an already multiplied estimate. The diameter does not imply a quadratic increase in thermal melt capacity.
+
+For INDX, combined infill uses the nozzle assigned to the current sparse or internal-solid surface and its configured maximum layer height. The existing multi-layer intersections and summed surface thickness provide the accumulated wall-layer height. The change avoids constraining sparse infill by an unrelated internal-solid nozzle, preserves perimeter layer heights, and leaves other printer behavior unchanged.
+
+Process layer_height and initial_layer_print_height adapt into a separate owned INDX process preset after the current plate is applied. Print::indx_layer_height_limit resolves the plate's region, volume/painting, height-range, support, tower and explicit tool-change filament assignments through the filament mapping. Only used tools constrain the shared Z schedule, using min(max_layer_height, 0.7 * nozzle_diameter). Empty plates have no usage limit. Model, assignment and hardware changes recalculate before validation/slicing and reapply the adapted process. Requested heights are restored when usage permits, unless manually changed. Combined sparse infill sums the base layer heights within its own assigned nozzle limit.
+
+
+The touchscreen hardware declaration uses schema 2, an explicit confirmation timestamp and an ordered tool list. It is never replaced by a received Orca catalogue. Printer preflight compares that declaration with TOOL_POSITIONS.tool_count, current Klipper readiness, commissioning flags and the UUID assigned to every used filament/tool pair. A project-side snapshot is evaluated during configuration apply; explicit inspection and sending fetch fresh state. Offline slicing/export remain available. INDX network sending is restricted to the current plate through the legacy Moonraker print-host path; the print-host URL must match the checked Moonraker URL. Other upload paths are not used for INDX. This is an operator declaration check, not physical tool detection.
+
+The confirmed touchscreen declaration owns tool count, nozzle diameters, Standard/CHT and the generic CHT factor. Orca imports it on its background polling cycle and before explicit filament synchronization. It validates the entire declaration before applying it through the same preset path as local nozzle editing, backs up the previous printer profile and recalculates the used-nozzle layer limits. Background changes wait while profiles have unsaved edits, slicing/export is active or a modal dialog is open. Results from a different connection or printer preset are discarded. Offline and unconfirmed responses do not resize a profile. The Ugelli button displays the printer-owned declaration when available; local editing remains a fallback when no confirmed declaration is reachable. Firmware dock positions and commissioning are independent of this synchronization.
+
+Calibration status is derived from per-nozzle records: verified requires a saved visual/manual test with matching temperature, bed, material, brand/product and effective flow where relevant. Estimated generic values and missing PA are distinct from stale measurements. Legacy numbers without complete provenance are retained but not applied as verified calibration. A PA or maximum-flow result becomes stale if its effective flow changes. Rename and colour edits preserve calibration identity. The GUI save paths require the matching generated test to have completed before storing a verified result.
+
+Generated preset fingerprints prevent background overwrites after local edits. Explicit synchronization displays changed values, offers cancellation, and backs up the previous generated configuration. Touchscreen JSON writes keep previous library, assignment, hardware and checklist files in a history folder. Installer backups restore code and menus without rolling back newer filament data.
+
+The role proposal uses the current filament/tool mapping and same-material candidate nozzles. Detail uses the finest associated nozzle up to 0.4 mm for walls and solid surfaces; balanced uses the largest up to 0.6 mm (or the smallest available fallback); speed uses the largest for walls, solids and sparse infill. Sparse infill uses the largest associated nozzle in every mode. Supports and painting remain explicit. Local feature overrides must be resolved before applying a global proposal. Preview movement properties show project tool/nozzle and the exact count of base Z intervals matching the deposited thickness, with no assumption of constant layer height. The manual commissioning checklist invalidates dependent steps after a failed prerequisite or changed hardware; it does not set Klipper movement gates.
+
+
+The INDX workspace uses direct selection buttons and a single content area in both applications. Orca reuses the selected physical printer host, the printer profile host/web UI address, or the migrated legacy host. It has no duplicate connection editor in the workspace. The three pages are Info INDX, Config tool and Auto slicing optimization. Hardware updates in place on Config tool, which also keeps explicit filament synchronization available. Optimization owns mode selection, native reslice, progress and print statistics. Selecting a different mode automatically applies and slices it; selecting an uncomputed mode also starts slicing. The shared action disables controls immediately during a pending run, and a Recalculate button allows retries without changing modes. Mode changes and invalidated results clear displayed metrics. Reapplying unchanged settings avoids duplicate process presets; generated names depend on settings rather than predecessor names, and edited presets are not overwritten. Native slicing validation and notifications remain active. The workspace does not switch the main view or initiate printing. Local edits to generated profiles still require review before replacement.
+
+KlipperScreen owns the machine editor. Incomplete coordinates may be saved as a draft. Applying requires a fresh idle snapshot, heaters off, complete bounded geometry and an unchanged file fingerprint. Only the INDX user configuration is edited; core tool-change macros are preserved. The editor generates missing Tn shortcuts and a revision-guarded delayed SAVE_VARIABLE block for offsets. That block runs once for a new revision, preserving later official calibration results. Every geometry save backs up files and invalidates previous commissioning. A maintenance installation can use an explicitly registered inactive reference profile for limits; it does not activate that profile.
+
+Explicit operator attestations in the machine editor can persist and enable measured docks for controlled tests, then geometry/cleaning for printing after the first six ordered physical checklist steps. They require the loaded revision, matching runtime geometry and an active INDX head/load-cell configuration. Installs, automatic synchronization and the checklist itself never enable these gates. Pickup and park remain existing vendor macro calls with the standard movement confirmation.
+
+La proposta tool usa i preset correnti e la mappatura del piatto corrente, senza dipendere dalla fotografia Print del processo in background. Dettaglio richiede pareti con ugello fino a 0,4 mm e seleziona quello piu fine associato. Per Dettaglio un ugello montato piu fine ma senza filamento associato blocca la proposta: non viene sostituito silenziosamente con uno piu grande. I filamenti caricati e il materiale devono essere dichiarati, non dedotti dal solo montaggio.
+
+Se esiste almeno uno slot per ogni tool e tutti gli slot hanno tipo materiale e colore identici, la proposta completa automaticamente una mappatura predefinita vuota, tutta sul primo tool o gestita da una modalita automatica Orca con la corrispondenza slot/tool. La mappatura esplicita non predefinita viene preservata. Il cambio si applica al progetto e al piatto soltanto insieme alla proposta, dopo snapshot; non dichiara caricamenti fisici o calibrazioni. Tutti i tool diventano candidati, senza imporre l'uso ridondante di ugelli equivalenti.
+
+Le proposte mantengono il riempimento sparso sul maggiore ugello disponibile in ogni stile. Dettaglio sceglie il piu fine fino a 0,4 mm per pareti e superfici. Equilibrato sceglie il maggiore fino a 0,6 mm per pareti e superfici, ricadendo sul minore disponibile se nessuno rispetta tale soglia. Tra tool dello stesso diametro viene preferito il primo senza introdurre cambi ridondanti per le funzioni fini. La griglia Z dipende soltanto dai tool delle funzioni effettivamente usate; l'infill combinato resta limitato dal suo ugello.
+
+Il controllo di compatibilita materiale INDX consulta la capacita del primo feeder fisico condiviso, anziche interpretare T3 o T63 come estrusori fisici distinti. Una capacita materiale assente resta un blocco; per le altre stampanti restano le capacita per estrusore, con shift fuori intervallo respinti.
+
+Veloce usa il maggiore ugello disponibile per pareti, superfici e riempimento sparso. Dettaglio ed Equilibrato mantengono i propri target per pareti e superfici. Con 0,4/0,6/0,8: Veloce seleziona 0,8 per tutte le funzioni proposte; gli ugelli piu piccoli montati ma inutilizzati non limitano la griglia Z. Supporti e pittura conservano le proprie assegnazioni esplicite.
+
+
+The optimization page keeps one result per mode for the lifetime of the workspace. Compare modes queues native slices sequentially and runs the initially selected mode last, preserving the user's final selection on successful completion. Only native valid results populate the table; time and weight badges are relative to computed rows. The comparison context hashes full configuration, plate mapping, nozzle setup, immutable mesh identity, instance/volume transformations, model settings and painting/layer profile revisions, and custom per-layer G-code. A role proposal explicitly accepts its own configuration transition; other context changes invalidate all rows. Unfinished, failed and interrupted runs never populate a row. The queue is cleared on errors or external edits. Cache invalidation also follows native result invalidation when idle.
+
+
+### Automatic comparison and measured foreign-infill benefit
+
+Entering optimization queues the three native slices once idle; the manual action reruns them. The current outer-wall assignment (slot 1 if automatic) defines the primary material. For each mode the workspace first slices the primary-material/color proposal, then larger infill candidates of another color or a documented conditional rigid/flexible pair. Release-only pairs (PLA/PETG), unsupported polymers and support-designated spools are excluded. Walls and solids remain primary. Only a valid positive saving at displayed whole-second resolution can produce a proposal; the fastest successful candidate is selected and the dialog shows both complete estimates, saved time/percentage and signed tool-change delta. Conditional infill approval discloses changes to flexibility and requires a formulation/adhesion test. Native INDX validation applies the same sourced model policy to structural slots, including local assignments, painting and custom tool changes; support contact is evaluated separately by role, not by the filament support flag. Unused loaded materials do not block a print. The default is No. Acceptance or refusal is followed by a final slice of the chosen variant, whose statistics alone populate the mode table.
+
+Trial phases retain a primary preset for restoration. Native errors or invalid estimates cannot generate a recommendation; failed trials restore the baseline. Project inputs are disabled while trials run; input changes invalidate the comparison and restore the primary process instead of accepting stale timing. A modal-choice guard prevents timer reentry. Closing waits until temporary trials and final restoration finish. No printer command is sent by this workflow. Homogeneous proposals retain the three-mode behavior without extra candidate trials.
+
+
+The material decision is aggregated: all three primary and alternative measurements complete before a single dialog lists time savings and signed tool-change deltas. Only modes with a positive saving receive an alternative on acceptance; decline keeps all primary variants. Decisions survive mode changes within the unchanged workspace and are reset by input invalidation or an explicit full comparison. Partial results cannot produce the decision. The GTK filament picker derives brand and material filters from the compatible source catalogue and personal library. Product metadata is selected rather than typed; a modal naming step precedes assignment, and cancellation does not write the library. Stable library identity is preserved on rename and recall.
+
+The touchscreen can assign an uncalibrated standard copy directly from an Orca catalogue base, or save editable values as a personal profile. Standard copies carry no test provenance. Calibration promotes a standard copy to a separate personal identity so the original base remains reusable. Selecting a color for the currently assigned personal/standard record saves only its color; other unsaved fields are not committed. Color changes retain valid calibration provenance.
+
+The background publisher includes assignments in the library fingerprint. Once idle and free of dirty presets or modal workflows, it persists generated filament copies and applies assigned tool slots, colors, temperatures and valid calibration values to the project. Extra unassigned project slots are preserved. Local generated-profile conflicts defer automatic application and remain available for explicit comparison. This is configuration synchronization, never a print command. The tool page exposes a visual swatch using Orca's existing palette dialog; color storage remains the compatible single-color project fields.
+
+
+### Bidirectional selection synchronization and touchscreen responsiveness
+
+The publisher establishes a printer baseline before exporting user changes. It observes selected preset identities and solid project colors through the current physical tool map. A unique assignment per physical tool is exported; conflicting virtual slots are excluded. Standard/user Orca presets become uncalibrated stock records on the printer. INDX identities recall an existing library record with its applicable calibration provenance. Changing one shared record's color creates a separate identity for that tool so another loaded tool does not change color unintentionally.
+
+A schema-1 request carries a unique request ID and each modified tool's expected assignment, complete library record and hardware declaration. The Linux metadata-only consumer compares all expected values under the same advisory lock used by touchscreen saves. Any mismatch rejects the whole batch; the Orca publisher pauses and exposes the conflict for explicit reviewed synchronization. Requests are idempotent. A write-ahead snapshot recovers interrupted library/assignment commits before later local writes. The consumer defers while printing or paused and never sends G-code. Its systemd unit starts independently of the active touch page and is included in the installer's backup/rollback handling. Cross-host wall clocks are not used to choose a winner.
+
+Orca waits for the matching acknowledgement and does not apply a stale response over a selection made during the network request. Import results become the next local baseline, preventing echo requests. A host, printer or reviewed-sync generation change discards stale responses. Offline requests keep their original expected base and cannot overwrite later touch edits.
+
+KlipperScreen caches parsed JSON by path, nanosecond mtime, size and inode; mutable reads are defensive copies. Catalogue snapshots are read-only and decompressed once per file identity. Dashboard pages stay inside a persistent Gtk.Stack with no transitions and non-homogeneous sizing. Overview cards and inactive page lists are rebuilt only when relevant files change. The visible filament page polls lightweight file revisions once a second and refreshes saved records only when no local form draft exists. Navigating away and back preserves unsaved fields. Overview cards display effective PA, flow ratio and maximum volumetric speed even when stock or estimated, separately labelled from verified calibrations.
+
+
+Automatic optimization processes and adaptive-Z variants are project-embedded presets. The workspace reuses a labelled slot for each mode and alternative, preserving the native project save/restore behavior without creating user-directory presets for every configuration hash. Existing non-project names are not overwritten. Permanent starting processes are grouped by nozzle diameter and requested layer height, separately from the three optimization modes. The used-nozzle cap still takes precedence; process presets do not change the hardware declaration or the model tool.
+
+
+The role-aware material table in IndxMaterials.hpp is a curated, dated snapshot with official source provenance. It is also exported through IndxChecks and the public JSON catalogue. Material/support guidance lists loaded slots and recommended absent interface types. Only recommended release/soluble pairs enable the interface action; unknown/conflicting evidence remains conditional. The action creates a project-embedded process, preserves filament settings, rejects local support overrides and shows the exact zero-top-gap/interface settings before applying. Qualified interfaces do not trigger the previous structural mixed-polymer error; unknown supports retain a targeted warning.
+
+
+Preset ownership hashes exclude the default filament color, which native 3MF import may rewrite; the effective project slot color remains part of the independent bidirectional selection comparison. Legacy hashes are accepted only through the prior normalization. Settings changes remain conflicts, with diagnostic field paths rather than configuration values logged. An unavailable material/nozzle mode is labelled unavailable; the comparison continues with the remaining modes without fabricating metrics or collecting an incomplete alternative-material decision.
